// EXPECT-DIAG: E130
// EXPECT-DIAG: Raw pointer type 'int*' cannot be used outside an 'unsafe' block
// Regression test (issue #238): a `to`-cast whose destination type is a
// raw pointer (`ptr`/`T*`) must be rejected outside `unsafe { }` -- this
// is the "cast" leg of the three explicitly gated uses (declaration,
// cast, dereference) FINAL_SPRINT.md's Track A specifies. `r` is a `&int`
// safe reference (no raw pointer declared anywhere) explicitly cast `to
// int*`: `typesCompatible()` already allows `&T -> T*` implicitly (see
// its Rule 2.5, src/Types.cpp), but an EXPLICIT cast still goes through
// the same gate check as any other cast involving a raw pointer type,
// regardless of whether the conversion itself would have been implicitly
// allowed -- the cast is checked (and fails) before `p`'s own
// raw-pointer-typed declaration would separately be gated too, since
// visitVarDecl() visits the initializer expression first. See also
// fail_ptr_outside_unsafe.tr,
// fail_struct_raw_ptr_field_outside_unsafe.tr, and
// fail_fn_signature_raw_pointer_disallowed.tr for the other three gated
// shapes, and 18_unsafe_block_and_safe_reference.tr for the corresponding
// passing cases.

fn main(): int {
    mutable v: int = 5;
    mutable r: &int = &v;
    mutable p: int* = r to int*;
    return 0;
}
